course: religion-ethics
lesson: 13
title: Jesus' life and teaching
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jesus began his work when John the Baptist baptised him in the river Jordan and a voice said «This is my Son, whom I love»; then for forty days in the desert he was tempted and said no each time, answering with Scripture.
explain: He called ordinary fishermen — Peter, Andrew, James and John — with «Follow me», and chose twelve apostles as different as a tax collector and a rebel.
explain: Asked for the greatest commandment, he said: love God with all your heart, soul and mind, and love your neighbour as yourself.
explain: He welcomed those others pushed away — children, tax collectors like Zacchaeus, people with leprosy, a Samaritan woman at a well — and their lives changed.
explain: For a child today this means: nobody is too small, too bad or too different to be welcomed, and saying sorry includes putting things right.
fact: John the Baptist baptised Jesus in the river Jordan, and a voice from heaven said «This is my Son, whom I love».
fact: For forty days in the desert Jesus was tempted by the devil and said no each time, using Scripture; Lent remembers these forty days.
fact: Jesus called fishermen such as Peter, Andrew, James and John, saying «Follow me and I will make you fishers of people».
fact: The twelve apostles were Jesus' closest followers, sent out to spread his message; they included Matthew the tax collector and Simon the Zealot.
fact: Jesus said the greatest commandment is to love God with all your heart, soul and mind, and the second is to love your neighbour as yourself.
fact: When the disciples sent children away, Jesus said «Let the children come to me».
fact: Zacchaeus was a short, dishonest tax collector who climbed a tree to see Jesus; after Jesus went to his house he paid back four times what he had cheated.
fact: Martha was busy serving while her sister Mary sat listening to Jesus, and Jesus said Mary had chosen the better part.
fact: Jesus ate with tax collectors and sinners and touched people with leprosy, whom others avoided.
fact: Jesus talked with a Samaritan woman at a well, whom others avoided, and offered her «living water».
fact: Jews and Samaritans of that time usually kept apart, which is why the woman was surprised Jesus spoke to her.
fact: Christians believe Jesus did no wrong, yet he joined ordinary people in being baptised.
exercise: Who baptised Jesus? | John the Baptist | Peter the fisherman | The high priest | Zacchaeus
exercise: In which river was Jesus baptised? | The Jordan | The Nile | The Euphrates | The Tigris
exercise: What did the voice from heaven say at Jesus' baptism? | This is my Son, whom I love | Go and build my Temple | Leave this land for Egypt | You must fast for forty days
exercise: How long was Jesus tempted in the desert? | Forty days | Three days | Seven days | Twelve days
exercise: How did Jesus answer each temptation? | He said no, using words of Scripture | He gave in once and then stopped and said sorry | He ran away back to Nazareth | He asked the disciples to decide
exercise: Which Christian season remembers Jesus' forty days in the desert? | Lent | Advent | Christmas | Pentecost
exercise: What job did Peter, Andrew, James and John do before following Jesus? | Fishermen | Carpenters | Tax collectors | Shepherds
exercise: Why do Christians find it striking that the apostles included a tax collector and a rebel? | Jesus brought very different people into one team | Jesus only chose rich and powerful men from the big cities | It shows the twelve never argued at all | They were all priests from the Temple
exercise: What did Jesus call the greatest commandment? | Love God with all your heart, soul and mind | Keep the Sabbath day holy above everything else you do | Never eat with tax collectors | Give a tenth of all your money
exercise: What is the second commandment Jesus joined to the first? | Love your neighbour as yourself | Honour the king and his officers in everything | Pray three times every day, facing the Temple | Wash your hands carefully before every meal
exercise: What did Jesus say when the disciples sent children away? | Let the children come to me | Children must wait until they are grown | Take the children home to their mothers | Only quiet children may stay
exercise: Why did Zacchaeus climb a tree? | He was short and wanted to see Jesus | He was hiding from the tax collectors | He wanted to pick figs for Jesus | He was told to by the crowd
exercise: What did Zacchaeus do after meeting Jesus? | Paid back four times what he had cheated | Became a fisherman on the lake with Peter and Andrew | Left the town and was never seen | Became the high priest
exercise: In the story of Mary and Martha, who had «chosen the better part»? | Mary, who sat listening to Jesus | Martha, who was busy cooking and serving the guests | Both equally, Jesus said, for both loved him | Neither, Jesus told them both off
exercise: How did Jesus treat people with leprosy? | He touched them and welcomed them | He sent them away from the town | He spoke to them only from far off | He told them to hide in caves
exercise: Why was the woman at the well surprised? | Jesus, a Jew, spoke to a Samaritan woman others avoided | Jesus asked her to pay him in gold and silver for the water | Jesus knew how to draw water himself | Jesus was riding a camel
exercise: What did Jesus offer the woman at the well? | Living water | A bag of silver | A new house | A place in the Temple
test: cousin: «Jesus said yes to the devil once in the desert, then stopped». right? | No, he said no every single time | Yes, he gave in to the first temptation | Yes, then the disciples saved him | No, he was never tempted at all
test: Atheist friend: «If Jesus did nothing wrong, why get baptised?» A fair Christian answer? | To join ordinary people at the start of his work | Because he had done many wrongs | Because John forced him into the river | Because baptism made him God's Son
test: «Zacchaeus just said sorry, that's all». What is missing from that retelling? | He put it right by paying back four times | He was sent to prison afterwards | He became one of the twelve apostles | Jesus refused to go to his house
test: Older brother: «Jesus only hung out with good church people, obviously». Put him right. | He ate with tax collectors and sinners others avoided | He only ever met priests and teachers inside the Temple | He refused to talk to Samaritans | He sent children away from crowds
test: ugh my little sis keeps tagging along, mates say tell her to get lost. what did Jesus do with kids | He welcomed them: «Let the children come to me» | He told them to go home and wait | He let only the oldest ones stay | He said children should not be seen
test: «Martha was lazy and Mary did all the work». What actually happened? | Martha was busy serving while Mary sat listening | Martha slept all afternoon while Mary cooked for everyone | Both sat listening and nobody served | Mary went out and Martha stayed home
test: There's a kid nobody sits with at lunch cos he «smells weird». What would follow Jesus' way with outcasts? | Sit with him and include him | Laugh along so you don't get left out | Tell a teacher to move him elsewhere | Ignore it, it's not your problem
test: Hindu classmate asks: «What is the main rule of Christianity in one line?» | Love God, and love your neighbour as yourself | Go to church every single Sunday, whatever happens | Never eat meat on any Friday | Read the whole Bible every year
test: Judge: «Why might Jesus have chosen fishermen rather than important people?» | He chose ordinary people and changed their lives | Fishermen were the richest men around | Important people had all said no first | Only fishermen could read the Scriptures
test: Which person in this lesson was a Samaritan? | The woman at the well | Zacchaeus the tax collector | Martha of Bethany | Peter the fisherman
test: «Love your neighbour as yourself» — what does it suggest about how you treat yourself? | You should value yourself too, and others as much | You should hate yourself so you can love others more | You should only love neighbours, not yourself | It says nothing at all about yourself
exam: Identify the location at which Jesus received baptism at the hands of John. | The river Jordan | The Sea of Galilee | The Temple in Jerusalem | The well of Samaria
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the commandment designated by Jesus as the greatest? | Love of God with the whole heart, soul and mind | Strict observance of the Sabbath rest | Payment of a tenth of one's income | Avoidance of all contact with sinners
exam: It is given that Zacchaeus, a collector of taxes, received Jesus into his house. Ascertain his subsequent conduct. | He restored fourfold that which he had wrongfully taken | He resigned his office and fled the town | He was received among the twelve apostles | He declined to make any restitution whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Christian season of Lent. | It commemorates forty days of Jesus' temptation in the desert | It commemorates the birth of Jesus in Bethlehem | It commemorates the coming of the Holy Spirit | It commemorates the journey of the magi
